                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bone Broth Protein Pure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.78</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Multi Collagen Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">35</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.99</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Multi Protein (Vanilla)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">26 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">135</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.83</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Protein (Unflavored)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $3.80</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            *At the Amazon prices we saw on November 21, 2026. Label figures are per Ancient Nutrition's supplement facts at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Ancient Nutrition Protein Powders, Label by Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            All four are sold by Ancient Nutrition on Amazon. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on November 21, 2026; label figures are per the brand's supplement facts at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Protein Powders Does Ancient Nutrition Sell?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ancient Nutrition sells four protein powder families: Bone Broth Protein, Multi Collagen Protein, Multi Protein and Whey Protein. The brand started with bone broth, and that is still the product most people mean when they search for Ancient Nutrition protein powder. The newer lines push the brand toward the mainstream shake market, with whey and milk protein doing most of the work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bone Broth Protein comes in a long list of flavors on the brand's site: Pure, Chocolate, Vanilla, Salted Caramel, Turmeric, Hot Cocoa and Pumpkin Spice, plus savory Chicken Soup, Tomato Basil and Butternut Squash. Most of the sweet and plain flavors list 20 g of protein per scoop. The savory soup flavors are different products in practice: the Chicken Soup label lists 15 g of protein, 2 g of fat and added tomato, carrot, garlic and herbs. The brand also lists a Bone Broth Protein Max tub in 40 servings, which shows the same 20 g and 90 calorie panel as Pure, and a separate Bone Broth Collagen line that the brand says gives 14 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Multi Collagen Protein is the brand's other big seller. It comes unflavored and in flavors such as Vanilla, Chocolate and Strawberry Lemonade, plus spin-offs with names like Beauty Within, Brain Boost and Gut Restore that add extra ingredients. It is closer to a collagen supplement than a protein powder, with 9 g of protein in a 10.1 g scoop of the unflavored version.
          </p>

          <p>
            Multi Protein is a newer five-source shake built on grass-fed whey, bone broth, A2/A2 milk protein, collagen peptides and colostrum, with 26 g of protein per scoop. A version with 5 g of creatine monohydrate costs a little more, and a plant-based Multi Protein with 25 g of protein per scoop uses pea and other plant proteins for vegan buyers. Finally, Whey Protein pairs grass-fed whey concentrate with A2/A2 milk protein for 23 g per scoop, and a Whey Protein + Fat Loss version adds ashwagandha and cinnamon. If you mainly want whey, our <a href="/best-grass-fed-whey-protein/">best grass-fed whey protein</a> guide covers cheaper options.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical point is that the four families answer four different questions. Bone broth is for dairy-free protein. Multi Collagen is for collagen. Multi Protein and Whey are for building muscle. Judging the whole brand on one product, as many reviews do, gives a misleading answer for the other three.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bone-broth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Ancient Nutrition Bone Broth Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ancient Nutrition Bone Broth Protein Pure contains 20 g of protein, 90 calories, 163 mg of sodium and 191 mg of potassium in a 22.3 g scoop, per the label at the time of writing. The ingredient list is a single Bone Broth Protein Blend made of chicken bone broth protein concentrate and beef bone broth protein concentrate. There is no sweetener, flavoring, gum or filler in the Pure version. The panel does not print carbohydrate, sugar or fat lines, although the brand's product page describes the line as 20 g of protein and 2 g of carbs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The flavored versions add a few ingredients. Chocolate uses a larger 25.2 g scoop that still lists 20 g of protein and 90 calories, with 2 g of carbs, 193 mg of sodium and 295 mg of potassium. Its other ingredients are natural chocolate flavor, organic cocoa powder processed with alkali, xanthan gum and stevia leaf extract. Vanilla follows the same pattern with stevia. None of the flavors we checked uses sucralose, acesulfame potassium or added sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein itself is the important caveat. Bone broth protein is mostly collagen-type protein, rich in glycine, proline and hydroxyproline but low in leucine, the amino acid most linked to muscle protein synthesis. Collagen also lacks tryptophan, so it is not a complete protein on its own. The brand highlights the collagen types, glucosamine, chondroitin and hyaluronic acid that occur naturally in broth, and those are the real reasons people buy it. If you want the full explanation of how this differs from whey, read our guide to <a href="/collagen-vs-protein-powder/">collagen vs protein powder</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergen-wise, Bone Broth Protein is free of dairy, soy, gluten and nuts, per the brand, and it is marketed as paleo and keto friendly. Every Ancient Nutrition label now carries the line that it is produced on equipment that also processes peanuts, tree nuts, milk, soybean, wheat, sesame, shellfish, fish and egg. The brand describes this as a voluntary disclaimer backed by allergen swab testing, but anyone with a severe allergy should treat it as a real cross-contact warning and check with their doctor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ancient Nutrition says its broth comes from pasture-raised, grass-fed and hormone-free animals, and it lists Carbon Neutral, Non-GMO tested and B Corp credentials on its product pages. Those are sourcing and company credentials rather than purity tests. You can see how the Pure label scores against other products on our <a href="/protein-powder/ancient-nutrition-bone-broth-protein/">Bone Broth Protein label page</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE LABEL IN ONE LINE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">20 g protein, 90 calories, two ingredients</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Bone Broth Protein Pure is chicken and beef bone broth protein concentrate and nothing else, per the label at the time of writing. That simplicity is its strength. Its weakness is that collagen-type protein does less for muscle than whey or a complete plant blend.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="multi-collagen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Multi Collagen Protein a Real Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Multi Collagen Protein is a collagen supplement, not a full protein powder: one 10.1 g scoop of the unflavored version gives 9 g of protein and 35 calories, per the label at the time of writing. The label lists 10 g of a Multi Collagen Complex, 90 mg of vitamin C from lipid metabolite ascorbate and a Bacillus coagulans probiotic at 2 billion CFU at the time of manufacture. The brand's own comparison describes it as giving 20 g of collagen per two scoops, which shows how it is meant to be used.
          </p>

          <p>
            The collagen comes from four sources: hydrolyzed bovine hide collagen peptides, fermented eggshell membrane, chicken bone broth protein concentrate and hydrolyzed fish collagen peptides from haddock, cod and pollock, per Ancient Nutrition. The brand markets this as 10 collagen types, from type I through type XXIII. That is accurate as a description of the sources, but your gut breaks every type down into the same pool of amino acids and small peptides, so the number of types matters far less than the dose.
          </p>

          <p>
            That dose question is where Multi Collagen sits well. Most collagen studies for skin and joints use roughly 2.5 to 15 g a day, and one or two scoops covers that range. The brand says most people take one scoop and some take up to three. What it will not do is replace a protein shake: 9 g of incomplete protein is a supplement, and if you count it toward a daily protein target you will overestimate how much useful protein you are eating.
          </p>

          <p>
            The allergen picture is also different from Bone Broth Protein. Because it contains fish collagen and eggshell membrane, Multi Collagen is not suitable for anyone with a fish or egg allergy, and it is not pescatarian or vegetarian. For alternatives with a larger collagen dose per scoop or a single source, see our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">best collagen protein powder</a> rankings, where Multi Collagen is judged against single-source peptides like Vital Proteins.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want collagen in your coffee and like the idea of vitamin C and a probiotic in the same scoop, Multi Collagen is a reasonable buy at around $32 for 45 servings at the Amazon price we saw. If you want protein for muscle, look further down this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="multi-protein-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What About Ancient Nutrition Multi Protein and Whey Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ancient Nutrition Multi Protein gives 26 g of protein in a 34.6 g scoop with 135 calories, 1.5 g of fat, 3 g of carbs and 2 g of sugar, per the vanilla label at the time of writing. The protein comes from a 32.8 g Multi Protein Animal Blend of grass-fed whey, chicken and beef bone broth, A2/A2 milk protein, collagen peptides and grass-fed colostrum. A 284 mg Protein Digestion Blend adds a protease enzyme and a Bacillus coagulans probiotic. The other ingredients are natural flavors, sea salt, stevia leaf extract and monk fruit extract.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because whey and milk protein are in the mix, Multi Protein is a complete protein with a useful amount of leucine, which bone broth and collagen are not. That makes it the only Ancient Nutrition product we would call a genuine all-purpose protein shake. The trade-off is that it contains milk, egg and fish, per its allergen statement, so it loses the dairy-free appeal that made the brand popular. The brand also advises against heating it, because whey and colostrum can clump and curdle in hot liquids.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Multi Protein + Creatine version keeps the same 26 g protein formula and adds 5 g of creatine monohydrate per scoop, the standard daily dose. At the Amazon price we saw, it cost about $6.70 more per 15-serving tub than plain Multi Protein. Buying creatine separately is almost always cheaper; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-creatine/">protein powder with creatine</a> guide runs that math.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey Protein Unflavored is the simpler option: 23 g of protein, 120 calories, 2 g of fat, 3 g of carbs and 2 g of sugar per scoop, per the brand's supplement facts at the time of writing. It combines grass-fed whey protein concentrate, organic regenerative A2/A2 nonfat milk protein and fermented eggshell membrane collagen, with no added flavors or sweeteners. The flavored versions add flavoring and sweetener, and the Fat Loss version adds ashwagandha and Ceylon cinnamon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both whey-based products are well formulated, with no sucralose or added sugar. Their problem is price, which the cost section below sets out. A standard grass-fed whey such as the ones in our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> guide gives similar protein for much less.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Ancient Nutrition Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ancient Nutrition says its products are tested, but none of its protein powders carries an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport certification that we could find. On its FAQ, the brand says products are tested for ingredient identification, purity, heavy metals and safety, and that its US manufacturing facilities are audited by third parties for current Good Manufacturing Practice compliance. It does not publish batch test results or heavy metal numbers on the product pages we read.
          </p>

          <p>
            The badges on the product pages are a different kind of claim. Bone Broth Protein lists Carbon Neutral Certified, Non-GMO Tested and B Corp under a 3rd Party Certified heading. Those are real credentials, but they cover carbon accounting, GMO status and company practices. None of them checks a tub for lead, banned substances or label accuracy, which is what most people mean when they ask whether a protein powder is third-party tested.
          </p>

          <p>
            This matters most for two groups. Athletes in tested sports should use products with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport status, because those programs screen every lot for banned substances; Ancient Nutrition does not offer that. People worried about heavy metals in bone broth and collagen products should know that animal bones and hides can carry trace metals, and without published results you are relying on the brand's word. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">best NSF certified protein powder</a> list is the place to start if a certified seal is a requirement.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, a large brand with audited facilities and a stated heavy metal testing program is better than a no-name tub with no claims at all. Treat it as a brand claim rather than independent proof. The same is true of most bone broth brands: our <a href="/paleovalley-bone-broth-protein-review/">Paleovalley bone broth protein review</a> found the same gap, and none of the five products in our bone broth roundup had an NSF or Informed Sport listing we could confirm.
          </p>
        </div>

