        <div id="buyer-reviews" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Do Buyers Say About Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly praise Bone Broth Protein Pure for being easy on the stomach and genuinely neutral in savory food. On Vitacost, where reviews are pooled with iHerb, 86 percent of the ratings we saw for the 2 lb Pure tub were five stars. Reviewers describe adding it to smoothies, muffins and soup, and several say it does not upset their gut the way dairy proteins do. One reviewer who reacts to casein called it their safe choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common complaint is flavor and texture in cold drinks. Pure tastes like a mild chicken broth, which suits soup but not a fruit smoothie, and some buyers say it is a little hard to mix. The brand itself recommends a frother or blender and says that mixability, taste, odor and color can vary between batches because it is made from broth concentrate. If you want a shake, buyers generally steer toward Chocolate or Vanilla, which are sweetened with stevia.
          </p>

          <p>
            Multi Collagen gets the best mixing feedback of the range. Reviewers say the unflavored powder dissolves fully in hot coffee and tea with no clumping, and most notice no change in taste. A minority report a faint protein-powder taste in plain tea or black coffee. On Vitacost, 81 percent of the ratings we saw for the 8.6 oz tub were five stars.
          </p>

          <p>
            Multi Protein and the whey line are too new to have a deep review history. The brand describes Multi Protein as lightly sweet without chalkiness, which is a marketing description rather than buyer evidence. The most common complaints across the range in reviews are about price and, for the bone broth line, the savory taste of Pure. We have not tasted these products; everything in this section is reported buyer feedback.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Ancient Nutrition vs Paleovalley, Vital Proteins and Beef Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ancient Nutrition Bone Broth Protein gives more protein per scoop than Paleovalley and usually costs less per gram. Paleovalley's bone broth protein lists about 13 to 15 g of protein per serving depending on flavor, against Ancient Nutrition's 20 g. Paleovalley's case is sourcing: it uses grass-fed beef bones only, while Ancient Nutrition blends chicken and beef. Our <a href="/paleovalley-bone-broth-protein-review/">Paleovalley review</a> covers that brand's label in full, and the <a href="/compare/ancient-nutrition-bone-broth-protein-vs-paleovalley-bone-broth-protein/">Ancient Nutrition vs Paleovalley comparison</a> puts the two labels side by side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against plain collagen peptides, the choice depends on what you want. Vital Proteins Collagen Peptides gives a bigger collagen dose per serving from a single bovine source, while Ancient Nutrition's Multi Collagen gives 9 to 10 g per scoop from four sources plus vitamin C and a probiotic. If you want bone broth rather than peptides, our <a href="/compare/ancient-nutrition-bone-broth-protein-vs-vital-proteins-collagen-peptides/">Bone Broth Protein vs Vital Proteins comparison</a> lays out the label differences.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a dairy-free animal protein for muscle, beef protein isolate is the stronger option. Products such as MuscleMeds Carnivor, covered in our <a href="/carnivor-protein-powder-review/">Carnivor protein powder review</a>, are built on beef protein isolate, a more concentrated protein than broth, while Designs for Sport's grass-fed beef protein is another single-source choice; see the <a href="/compare/ancient-nutrition-bone-broth-protein-vs-designs-for-sport-grass-fed-beef-protein/">Ancient Nutrition vs Designs for Sport comparison</a>. Our <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">best beef protein powder</a> guide ranks the field.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against whey, Ancient Nutrition's own whey and Multi Protein lose on price. Single-ingredient grass-fed whey from brands in our <a href="/best-grass-fed-whey-protein/">grass-fed whey</a> guide gives similar protein per scoop for less than half the cost per gram. The reason to choose Ancient Nutrition's version is the extra ingredients, colostrum, A2/A2 milk protein, collagen and enzymes, and whether those are worth paying for is a personal call rather than something the research settles.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a broader view of the brand, including how its products sit in our label database, see the <a href="/brands/ancient-nutrition/">Ancient Nutrition brand page</a>. For other dairy-free options, our <a href="/best-dairy-free-protein-powder/">best dairy-free protein powder</a> and <a href="/best-paleo-protein-powder/">best paleo protein powder</a> guides cover alternatives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Who Should Buy Ancient Nutrition, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Ancient Nutrition Bone Broth Protein if you want a dairy-free, soy-free protein with a short label and you like the idea of cooking with it. It is a natural fit for paleo and keto eaters, people who react to whey or casein, and anyone who would rather add 20 g of protein to a bowl of soup than drink a shake. The 40-serving Pure tub is the version that makes the most sense on price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Multi Collagen if your aim is collagen for skin, hair or joints and you want something that disappears into your morning coffee. Count it as a supplement, not as protein. Buy Multi Protein only if you specifically want the colostrum, enzymes and five-source formula and accept paying close to double a regular whey for them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip the brand if your main goal is building muscle on a budget. Whey or a complete plant blend will give you more leucine for less money. Skip it if you compete in a tested sport, because no Ancient Nutrition protein carries an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport seal that we could find. Skip Multi Collagen and Multi Protein if you have a fish or egg allergy, and skip Multi Protein and Whey if you avoid dairy.
          </p>

          <p>
            A health note: protein powders are food supplements, not medicine. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, take regular medication or are an older adult with a medical condition, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a protein or collagen supplement. Older adults looking for an easy protein boost can also compare options in our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-seniors/">best protein powder for seniors</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

