          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting Multi Collagen as a protein shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A scoop of Multi Collagen gives 9 g of incomplete protein. It supports a collagen routine, but if you log it as a protein shake you will think you are eating far more useful protein than you are. Pair it with complete protein from food, whey or a plant blend if muscle is the goal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the savory flavors for protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chicken Soup and Tomato Basil look like Bone Broth Protein but list about 15 g of protein, not 20 g, plus fat, salt and vegetables. They are good sipping broths. If protein per scoop is why you are buying, stick to Pure, Chocolate or Vanilla and check the panel first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming B Corp means lab tested</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 3rd Party Certified badges on Ancient Nutrition pages are Carbon Neutral, Non-GMO Tested and B Corp. None of them tests for lead or banned substances. If you need that assurance, look for NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, which this brand does not carry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the small tub</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At the Amazon prices we saw, the 20-serving Bone Broth Protein Pure worked out to about $2.39 per 25 g of protein, against about $1.78 for the 40-serving tub. Unless you are trying it for the first time, the larger tub saves around a quarter on every gram of protein.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Missing the fish and egg in the blends</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bone Broth Protein is free of the major allergens apart from shared equipment, but Multi Collagen contains fish collagen and eggshell membrane, and Multi Protein lists milk, egg and fish. People with those allergies should read the Contains line on each label rather than assuming the whole brand is allergen-friendly.
              </p>
            </div>
